source: crates/agent-api/src/routines/prompt.rs
expression: "generate_prompt(&routine(Runner::Local), PromptTarget::DryRun)"
---
This is a preview. Do not change anything: do not create labels, apply labels or archive. Classify the candidate threads and reply with the result only.

Sort the user's mail matching `is:important newer_than:1d -in:sent -in:draft` into cadence-based review labels under “Marked Important”, so nothing important is lost but the inbox stays clear.

Use OpenAGC's mail tools (mail_list_labels, mail_search, mail_get_thread, mail_create_label, mail_add_label, mail_archive). The user is me@example.com. They also receive mail at me@work.example and me@side.example. They are frequently CC'd on mail to team@work.example.

## Step 1 — Find candidates

Search with mail_search: `is:important newer_than:1d -in:sent -in:draft`

Threads that already carry one of the 6 labels (`Marked Important/1-Daily`, `Marked Important/2-Weekly-Newsletters`, `Marked Important/3-Weekly-Events`, `Marked Important/4-Weekly-Finance`, `Marked Important/5-Monthly-Pitches`, and `Marked Important/6-Weekly-Hiring`) were sorted on an earlier run. A thread can come back because a newer message arrived: leave those out of the preview.

Page with next_cursor, but stop after 200 threads — if you hit that cap, say so rather than silently truncating. Classify from sender, subject and snippet. Only call mail_get_thread when the snippet genuinely isn't enough to decide — it is slow and most classifications are obvious from the sender.

## Step 2 — Leave these alone

Do NOT label and do NOT archive:

- **Genuine person-to-person threads** — a real human writing to the user or a small group they are part of, about actual work: introductions, deal threads, calendar invites from named people, event logistics with named collaborators, replies to their own mail. When in doubt about whether a thread is human correspondence, leave it in the inbox.
- Any thread the user has already replied to (it contains a message from me@example.com, me@work.example, and me@side.example).
- Starred threads.
- Anything in Spam or Trash.

The principle behind these rules: wrongly deferring a real conversation is much worse than leaving one extra message in the inbox.

## Step 3 — Sort the rest

Decide which one label each remaining thread would get:

**`Marked Important/1-Daily`** — Automated mail that needs a human action soon: service and infrastructure alerts (outages, end-of-support notices, security warnings), failed or declined payments, expiring cards, account suspension warnings, deadlines within about a week, legal or compliance notices, and anything marked "Action Required" that is genuinely actionable. For example: cloud provider health and outage alerts, "your payment was declined", and "your card expires this month". When something is both an alert and a receipt, prefer this label.

**`Marked Important/2-Weekly-Newsletters`** — Newsletters, Substack and similar sends, mailing-list digests, keyword-alert bots, industry news roundups, and vendor product-marketing announcements the user did not personally ask about. Reading material, no action. For example: Substack and beehiiv newsletters, Google Groups digests, and vendor product-update announcements.

**`Marked Important/3-Weekly-Events`** — Event invitations and logistics from platforms: meetups, conferences, summits, webinars, demo days, ticketing and RSVP-platform invites, RSVP-activity and post-event survey notifications, early-bird deadlines. For example: Eventbrite and Luma invitations, conference early-bird reminders, and webinar registrations. Event mail from a named human collaborator about an event the user is co-hosting is person-to-person correspondence: leave it in the inbox.

**`Marked Important/4-Weekly-Finance`** — Receipts, invoices, billing statements, payment confirmations, subscription renewals, bank and card statements, expense-tool and travel-spend reports, routine payment-account notices. Successful and routine only. For example: "your receipt from …", monthly card statements, and subscription renewal confirmations. Anything failed or overdue belongs in 1-Daily.

**`Marked Important/5-Monthly-Pitches`** — Cold outbound from vendors and founders, sales follow-ups ("just following up", "one last message"), recruiting-service and candidate-sourcing blasts, sponsorship and expo solicitations, unsolicited demo requests. Low priority, batch-review monthly. For example: "just following up on my last note", sponsorship packages, and "can I get 15 minutes on your calendar?".

**`Marked Important/6-Weekly-Hiring`** — Inbound hiring pipeline for the user's own open roles: automated new-application notifications, applicant-tracking and job-board notices about candidates who applied, interview-scheduling bot mail. These are people who applied and deserve a real weekly pass. For example: "new application for …" from an applicant-tracking system and job-board candidate notifications. Recruiting vendors selling sourcing services or blasting candidate lists are cold outreach: 5-Monthly-Pitches. A named candidate or hiring collaborator writing directly is person-to-person correspondence: leave it in the inbox. Direction matters: candidates coming in belong here; vendors selling in do not.

Anything that fits none of these and is clearly automated: leave it untouched and mention it in the report so the buckets can be adjusted over time.

## Result

Reply with only a JSON array, one object per candidate thread: `{"thread_id": "…", "bucket": one of ["daily", "newsletters", "events", "finance", "pitches", "hiring"] or null to leave it, "reason": "a few words"}`. No other text.
